1. STORE API DESIGN
===================

Implement a small, explicit internal/store API for persisting already-parsed
chain.Block / chain.Transaction data.

Keep SQL concerns inside internal/store.

The caller should not need to know table ordering or PostgreSQL details.

A reasonable shape may include concepts such as:

  Store
  ApplyBlock(...)
  Tip(...)
  RollbackTo(...)
  GetUTXO(...)
  ...

but inspect the existing Go models and choose the smallest clean API.

Do not create an oversized repository abstraction or ORM.


2. ONE DATABASE TRANSACTION PER BLOCK
=====================================

Applying one canonical block must happen inside ONE PostgreSQL transaction.

All of these operations belong to that same transaction:

- block row
- immutable transaction bodies
- transaction_variants
- block_transactions
- transaction_inputs
- witness items
- transaction_outputs
- output_addresses
- output_participants
- canonical UTXO state
- address-cache recomputation
- sync_state checkpoint

The sync_state update must be the FINAL logical write.

If anything fails before commit:

  NOTHING from that block may persist.


3. IDEMPOTENT BLOCK APPLICATION
===============================

Reapplying the exact same already-indexed block must be safe.

Do not use blind additive operations.

Use natural keys / semantic ON CONFLICT behavior.

For immutable records:

- existing identical data is acceptable
- conflicting data for the same immutable identity must return an error

Do NOT silently overwrite contradictory immutable blockchain data.

Examples:

same txid + identical body:
  acceptable

same txid + different version/locktime/body:
  ERROR

same wtxid + identical variant:
  acceptable

same wtxid + contradictory txid/metrics:
  ERROR

same block hash + contradictory header data:
  ERROR


4. TXID / WTXID PRESERVATION
=============================

Respect the reviewed schema exactly.

transactions:
  txid-based non-witness body

transaction_variants:
  wtxid-based full observed serialization metadata

block_transactions:
  exact txid/wtxid occurrence

transaction_input_witness:
  wtxid-specific witness stack

Never key witness persistence by txid alone.

A transaction without witness still gets a transaction_variants row with:

  wtxid == txid


5. RAW INPUT / OUTPUT PRESERVATION
==================================

Persist every vin and every vout one-for-one.

Do not:

- aggregate same-address inputs
- aggregate outputs
- drop zero-value outputs
- drop OP_RETURN/nulldata
- truncate scripts
- truncate witness items

Preserve raw BYTEA exactly.

A P2QPK 17,088-byte signature must round-trip exactly.


6. SCRIPT CLASSIFICATION OUTPUT
===============================

The store should accept the already-classified Phase-2A output model.

Persist:

- script_type
- witness_version
- witness_program
- raw scriptPubKey
- destination address when applicable
- bare-multisig participants when applicable

Respect:

output_addresses
  balance destination only

output_participants
  multisig search/display identities only

Never use output_participants for monetary aggregation.


7. CANONICAL UTXO APPLICATION
=============================

When applying a canonical block:

For every new output:

- create/update the appropriate utxo_state row
- creation block must be this block

For every non-coinbase input:

- locate the previous canonical UTXO
- ensure it exists and is currently unspent
- mark it spent by THIS exact spending tx/vin/block

A double-spend against the current canonical state must fail the entire
block transaction.

Do not invent missing previous outputs.

For historical sequential sync, previous outputs should already exist.


8. FEES
=======

For each non-coinbase transaction, once all prevout values are available:

  fee = sum(inputs' previous output values) - sum(outputs)

Use integer satoshis only.

No float64.

Reject negative computed fees as an internal/indexing inconsistency.

Coinbase:

  fee_satoshis = NULL

Do NOT infer issued supply from coinbase total.

The coinbase output total includes subsidy + transaction fees.


9. ADDRESS CACHE — SET BASED ONLY
=================================

This is critical because it is where the old Eiquidus explorer corrupted
balances after crash/replay.

Never use additive:

  balance = balance + ...
  total_received += ...
  $inc-style logic

After canonical state changes, recompute every TOUCHED address from the
source tables and SET the resulting absolute values.

Use:

  output_addresses

never:

  output_participants

as the monetary-address relation.

Recompute at minimum:

- total_received_satoshis
- total_sent_satoshis
- balance_satoshis
- tx_count
- first_seen_height
- last_seen_height

Define the exact semantics in ARCHITECTURE.md and tests.


10. CHECKPOINT
==============

Only after all block state and address caches are correct:

update:

  sync_state(name='main')

to the block hash.

The database trigger will derive height and verify that the block is
canonical.

This must be the final logical operation before commit.


11. REORG STORE ENGINE
======================

Implement store-layer rollback/reorg operations using the frozen architecture.

Do not fetch blocks from Core yet.

The store operation should be testable with synthetic already-persisted
fork data.

For a rollback to common ancestor:

- old canonical blocks above ancestor become canonical=false
- orphaned_at is set

Immutable audit data remains:

- blocks
- transactions
- transaction_variants
- block_transactions
- inputs
- witnesses
- outputs

Canonical derived state must be reconstructed correctly:

- utxo_state
- addresses
- sync_state

Do not delete immutable orphan history.

After rollback, sync_state must reference the ancestor canonical block.

Design the implementation so the future indexer can:

1. detect fork
2. ask store to rollback to ancestor
3. apply replacement blocks normally


12. REORG DEPTH POLICY
======================

Preserve the architecture policy:

- reorg depth <= 100:
    automatic store/indexer handling

- reorg depth > 100:
    future indexer must halt for operator intervention

The STORE may expose enough information to calculate rollback depth, but do
not build the live indexer policy loop in this phase.


13. REQUIRED DATABASE TESTS
===========================

Use the existing isolated PostgreSQL test infrastructure.

Add strong integration tests.

At minimum prove:

A. single block applies atomically

B. exact same block replay is idempotent

C. replay does not increase address balances/counts

D. contradictory immutable block data is rejected

E. contradictory txid body is rejected

F. contradictory wtxid variant is rejected

G. zero-value nulldata output survives

H. 17,088-byte P2QPK witness survives through ApplyBlock

I. two wtxid variants sharing one txid remain distinct

J. normal spend marks exact previous UTXO spent

K. attempting to spend an already-spent canonical UTXO rejects whole block

L. nonexistent prevout rejects whole block

M. fee is computed exactly in satoshis

N. coinbase fee stays NULL

O. multisig participants never multiply address balance

P. intentional failure BEFORE checkpoint rolls back every write from block

Q. intentional failure after some tx/output/UTXO work but before commit
   leaves sync_state and all canonical data unchanged

R. address cache after replay equals cache before replay exactly

S. simple one-block reorg restores previously spent UTXO correctly

T. multi-block reorg restores all canonical UTXOs correctly

U. orphan block/tx/witness/output audit data remains queryable after rollback

V. sync_state ends at the common ancestor after rollback

W. replacement branch can then be applied normally

X. after reorg + replacement, address balances equal a fresh recomputation
   from canonical source tables


